<p>An office cleaning checklist splits the work by how often it needs doing. Daily tasks cover bins, desks, kitchens, toilets and high-touch points; weekly tasks cover full hoovering, dusting and descaling; monthly and periodic tasks cover fridges, high-level dusting and deep cleaning carpets and upholstery.</p>
<p>Getting that split right keeps the workplace clean and healthy without paying for work that doesn't need doing every day. It also means the jobs nobody sees, like the tops of kitchen cupboards or the undersides of desks, don't get forgotten for a year.</p>
<p>Below is the checklist our cleaners use as a starting point for offices across Hull and East Yorkshire, from small suites in the Old Town to business units and surgeries in the market towns. Adapt it to your headcount, layout and the way your team uses the space.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>High-touch points such as door handles, kettles, taps and shared keyboards need daily attention, not weekly.</li><li>Kitchens and toilets are what staff and visitors judge first, so build the schedule around them.</li><li>Weekly and monthly tasks stop dust, limescale and carpet soiling turning into a bigger, costlier job.</li><li>Write the checklist down, agree who does what, and sign off each visit so nothing quietly drops off the list.</li></ul></div>
<h2>Why every office needs a written cleaning checklist</h2>
<p>Without a written list, office cleaning drifts towards the obvious jobs. Bins get emptied and floors get hoovered, but phone handsets, chair arms and the fridge get missed for months, and nobody notices until there's a smell or a complaint.</p>
<p>A checklist also helps you meet your legal duty. Under the Workplace (Health, Safety and Welfare) Regulations 1992, workplaces, furniture and fittings must be kept sufficiently clean. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:cleaning-regulations-for-businesses}}">workplace cleaning and the law</a> explains what that means in practice.</p>
<p>If you use a contractor for <a href="{{service:office-cleaning}}">professional office cleaning</a>, the checklist becomes your cleaning specification. It's what you agree up front, what you check the work against, and what gets put right if something is missed.</p>
<h2>Daily office cleaning tasks</h2>
<p>Daily tasks are about hygiene and first impressions. In a small, lightly used office some of these can be done every other day, but kitchens, toilets and touch points should be cleaned daily whenever people are working in the building.</p>
<h3>Desks and workstations</h3>
<ul>
<li>Wipe clear desk surfaces, working around papers and personal items rather than moving them.</li>
<li>Wipe shared phones, keyboards and mice, especially on hot desks.</li>
<li>Empty under-desk bins and recycling, and replace the liners.</li>
<li>Return stray mugs and plates to the kitchen.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Kitchen and break areas</h3>
<ul>
<li>Clean and disinfect worktops, tables and chair backs.</li>
<li>Wipe the kettle, microwave (inside and out), toaster, coffee machine and fridge handle.</li>
<li>Clean the sink, taps and draining board, and load or empty the dishwasher.</li>
<li>Empty food waste, general waste and recycling.</li>
<li>Sweep and mop the floor.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Toilets and washrooms</h3>
<ul>
<li>Clean and disinfect toilets, seats, urinals and flush handles.</li>
<li>Clean sinks, taps, mirrors and splashbacks.</li>
<li>Refill soap, toilet roll and paper towels, and empty sanitary and waste bins.</li>
<li>Mop the floor, paying attention to the area around the base of each toilet.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Entrances, floors and touch points</h3>
<ul>
<li>Hoover carpeted walkways and mop hard floors in reception and corridors.</li>
<li>Remove fingerprints and smears from glass entrance doors.</li>
<li>Wipe door handles, push plates, light switches, lift buttons, handrails and the reception desk.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Weekly office cleaning tasks</h2>
<p>Weekly jobs stop dust and grime building up to the point where a deep clean is needed. If your office is only cleaned once or twice a week, fold some of these into each visit on a rotation.</p>
<ul>
<li>Hoover all carpets fully, including under desks, along edges and behind doors.</li>
<li>Dust skirting boards, window sills, radiators, low cabinet tops and picture frames.</li>
<li>Dust monitors and desk equipment with a dry microfibre cloth.</li>
<li>Wipe chair arms, bases and the hard parts of meeting room chairs.</li>
<li>Clean internal glass partitions and meeting room whiteboards (with permission).</li>
<li>Descale washroom taps, shower screens and the kettle.</li>
<li>Wash bins inside and out to stop smells building up.</li>
</ul>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Much of Hull and East Yorkshire has hard water, so limescale builds up quickly on washroom taps, kettles and coffee machines. Our <a href="{{guide:hard-water-limescale-east-yorkshire}}">hard water and limescale guide</a> covers what works and what can damage chrome.</p></div>
<h2>Monthly and periodic office cleaning tasks</h2>
<p>These are the jobs that make the difference between an office that looks clean and one that actually is. Most can be scheduled monthly; some only need doing a few times a year.</p>
<h3>Monthly</h3>
<ul>
<li>Clear and clean the fridge. Give staff a day's notice so nobody's lunch is thrown away by mistake.</li>
<li>Wipe inside kitchen cupboards and the tops of wall units.</li>
<li>Dust high-level areas: light fittings, air vents, tops of tall cupboards and door frames.</li>
<li>Spot-clean upholstered chairs and soft seating.</li>
<li>Deep clean washroom grout, vents and cubicle partitions.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Periodic</h3>
<ul>
<li>Deep clean carpets, ideally by hot water extraction. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:carpet-cleaning-methods-explained}}">hot water extraction and dry carpet cleaning</a> explains why it lifts more embedded dirt.</li>
<li>Clean upholstered reception seating and fabric office chairs.</li>
<li>Degrease the kitchen thoroughly, including behind and under appliances.</li>
<li>Wash internal windows and window frames.</li>
</ul>
<p>Periodic work like this is often booked as a separate visit, out of hours, so it doesn't disrupt the team. Our <a href="{{service:carpet-upholstery-cleaning}}">carpet and upholstery cleaning</a> can be scheduled alongside your regular clean.</p>
<h2>Office cleaning schedule at a glance</h2>
<p>Use this table as a quick-reference version of the checklist, or as the starting point for your own schedule.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Area</th><th>Daily</th><th>Weekly</th><th>Monthly or periodic</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Desks and workstations</td><td>Wipe clear surfaces, empty bins</td><td>Dust monitors, wipe chairs</td><td>Spot-clean upholstered chairs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kitchen</td><td>Worktops, sink, appliances, waste</td><td>Inside microwave, descale kettle</td><td>Clear the fridge, clean inside cupboards</td></tr>
<tr><td>Toilets and washrooms</td><td>Clean, disinfect and restock</td><td>Descale taps, detailed floor clean</td><td>Grout, vents and partitions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Floors</td><td>Hoover walkways, mop hard floors</td><td>Full hoover including edges</td><td>Carpet deep clean</td></tr>
<tr><td>Touch points</td><td>Handles, switches, lift buttons</td><td>Handrails, chair arms</td><td>Light fittings, high-level dusting</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2>How to tailor an office cleaning checklist to your building</h2>
<p>No two offices are the same. A small accountancy practice in a converted townhouse has different needs from a call centre with shift workers, or a clinic with members of the public coming through the door all day.</p>
<ol>
<li>Walk the building and list every room and area, including stairs, store rooms and the bin store.</li>
<li>Note how each area is used and roughly how many people use it.</li>
<li>Decide the frequency for each task: daily, weekly, monthly or periodic.</li>
<li>Agree what staff do (clearing desks, washing their own mugs) and what the cleaners do.</li>
<li>Add a sign-off sheet so each visit is recorded.</li>
<li>Review the checklist every few months, and after any change such as new starters, hot-desking or a refit.</li>
</ol>
<p>If your team hot-desks, or you've had a run of colds and stomach bugs, increase the frequency of touch-point cleaning. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:office-kitchen-fridge-hygiene}}">reducing the spread of germs at work</a> covers this in more detail.</p>
<h2>Common office cleaning mistakes to avoid</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Using the same cloths everywhere.</strong> Colour-code cloths and mops so anything used in the toilets never goes near the kitchen.</li>
<li><strong>Spraying liquid directly onto electronics.</strong> Spray the cloth, not the keyboard or screen.</li>
<li><strong>Forgetting the fridge and microwave.</strong> These are often the grubbiest places in an office kitchen.</li>
<li><strong>Storing chemicals carelessly.</strong> Keep cleaning products in their original containers with safety data sheets to hand, in line with <a href="https://www.hse.gov.uk/coshh/" rel="noopener">HSE guidance on COSHH</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Cutting frequency too far.</strong> Fewer visits look like a saving, but dirt that builds up for weeks takes longer to shift. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:office-cleaning-cost-factors}}">what affects the cost of office cleaning</a> explains the trade-offs.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Getting your office cleaning checklist done properly</h2>
<p>A checklist only works if someone follows it every time. Our team works from a written specification agreed with you before we start, with a fixed quote, vetted and DBS-checked cleaners, and out-of-hours visits so cleaning doesn't get in the way of work.</p>
