<p>The most effective way to reduce the spread of germs at work is good workplace hygiene built on three things: regular cleaning of high-touch surfaces, easy access to proper handwashing, and a culture where people with stomach bugs or flu stay at home. Cleaning on its own won't stop every cold, but a workplace that gets all three right gives viruses far fewer chances to pass from person to person.</p>
<p>Winter is when most offices notice it, as colds, flu and norovirus work their way round a team. This guide covers the practical steps our cleaners see making a difference in offices across Hull and East Yorkshire, and what employers can do beyond the cleaning rota.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Germs spread mostly via hands and shared surfaces, so focus on high-touch points every day.</li><li>Clean first, then disinfect: disinfectant works poorly on a dirty surface.</li><li>Kitchens and washrooms need the most attention, and soap and paper towels should never run out.</li><li>Encourage staff with stomach bugs or flu to stay at home, and clean thoroughly after an outbreak.</li></ul></div>
<h2>How germs spread in the workplace</h2>
<p>Most common workplace illnesses spread in a few predictable ways. Someone coughs or sneezes, droplets land on a desk or door handle, and the next person touches that surface and then their face. Stomach bugs such as norovirus spread easily through contaminated hands, surfaces and shared food.</p>
<p>Offices make this easy. People share kettles, fridges, printers, meeting room tables and door handles, and many workplaces now hot-desk. One unwell person can touch dozens of surfaces before lunchtime.</p>
<p>That's why good workplace hygiene focuses on the points where hands meet surfaces, rather than on making everything look spotless.</p>
<h2>High-touch surfaces to clean every day</h2>
<p>High-touch points should be cleaned at least daily in any occupied office, and more often during an outbreak. Our <a href="{{service:office-cleaning}}">office cleaning</a> visits include these as standard:</p>
<ul>
<li>Door handles, push plates and light switches</li>
<li>Kettle handles, fridge doors, microwave buttons and coffee machine controls</li>
<li>Taps, flush handles and soap dispenser levers</li>
<li>Lift buttons, handrails and entry keypads</li>
<li>Printer and photocopier control panels</li>
<li>Shared phones, keyboards and mice</li>
<li>Meeting room tables and chair arms</li>
</ul>
<p>For the full list of daily, weekly and monthly tasks, see our <a href="{{guide:office-cleaning-checklist}}">office cleaning checklist</a>.</p>
<h3>Hot-desking and shared equipment</h3>
<p>Hot-desking means more hands on the same keyboard, mouse and desk surface. Keep a supply of suitable wipes at each bank of desks and ask staff to wipe down when they arrive and when they leave. A clear-desk policy helps too: cleaners can wipe a clear desk properly, but they shouldn't move papers or personal belongings.</p>
<h2>Cleaning vs disinfecting: what's the difference?</h2>
<p>Cleaning removes dirt, grease and many germs from a surface using detergent and water. Disinfecting uses a product designed to kill the germs that remain. Both matter, and so does the order: disinfectant works poorly on a surface that's still dirty, so clean first, then disinfect.</p>
<ul>
<li>Use a product labelled as a disinfectant for toilets, taps and touch points.</li>
<li>Follow the contact time on the label. Many disinfectants need to stay wet on the surface for a set time to work.</li>
<li>Never mix products, especially bleach with other cleaners.</li>
<li>Store chemicals safely and keep safety data sheets, as required under <a href="https://www.hse.gov.uk/coshh/" rel="noopener">COSHH</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Everyday cleaning of desks, floors and most surfaces doesn't need harsh chemicals. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:gentle-cleaning-hard-water-homes}}">eco-friendly cleaning that actually works</a> explains where gentler products do the job well and where a disinfectant is the better choice.</p>
<h2>Office kitchens and washrooms: the germ hotspots</h2>
<h3>Kitchens</h3>
<p>Office kitchens are shared by everyone and owned by no one. Sponges and dishcloths left damp in the sink can harbour bacteria, so replace them often or switch to cloths that go through a hot wash. Wipe and disinfect worktops, handles and the sink daily, and clear the fridge regularly so out-of-date food doesn't pile up.</p>
<h3>Washrooms</h3>
<p>Washrooms need daily cleaning and disinfection, with particular attention to flush handles, taps, cubicle locks and the inside door handle. Just as important, soap and paper towels must never run out. If people can't wash and dry their hands properly, they won't.</p>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Put a hand sanitiser dispenser by the kitchen door and the main entrance. It won't replace handwashing, but it catches people at two of the points where germs are most likely to be passed on.</p></div>
<h2>Hand hygiene, sickness policy and staff habits</h2>
<p>Even the best cleaning schedule can't keep up with poor habits. A few simple measures make a real difference:</p>
<ul>
<li>Remind staff to wash their hands with soap and water for at least 20 seconds, particularly after using the toilet and before eating, in line with NHS advice.</li>
<li>Encourage people to cough or sneeze into a tissue, bin it and then wash their hands.</li>
<li>Support staff with diarrhoea or vomiting to stay off until 48 hours after their symptoms have stopped, which is the NHS guidance for stomach bugs.</li>
<li>Where the role allows, make it acceptable to work from home with a heavy cold.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Ventilation, dust and air quality</h3>
<p>Fresh air helps dilute airborne germs, so open windows where you can and keep ventilation systems maintained. Dust and mould also affect people with allergies and asthma, and they're a common problem in older buildings such as the converted offices in Hull's Old Town. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:bedroom-dust-mite-reduction}}">cleaning for allergies and asthma</a> covers dust, pollen and mould in more detail.</p>
<h2>What to do after an outbreak of illness at work</h2>
<p>When a stomach bug or flu has been going round the office, a thorough one-off clean helps stop it bouncing back. That means cleaning and disinfecting washrooms, kitchens, desks, chairs, phones and every touch point, not just the usual daily tasks.</p>
<ol>
<li>Clean and disinfect toilets, sinks, taps and washroom floors first.</li>
<li>Disinfect every touch point listed above, plus shared equipment.</li>
<li>Clear the fridge, wash crockery on a hot cycle and replace cloths and sponges.</li>
<li>Wipe desks, chair arms and keyboards, including hot desks.</li>
<li>Keep up the extra frequency for a week or two after people return.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="{{service:commercial-cleaning}}">commercial cleaning</a> team can carry out this kind of clean out of hours so the office is ready for the next morning. Keeping on top of hygiene also helps you meet your duties under the Workplace Regulations, explained in our guide to <a href="{{guide:cleaning-regulations-for-businesses}}">workplace cleaning and the law</a>.</p>
<h2>Improving workplace hygiene all year round</h2>
<p>Good workplace hygiene isn't a winter-only job. A consistent schedule, the right products and staff who know what's expected of them will do more than any one-off blitz.</p>
<p>We provide <a href="{{local:office-cleaning-hull}}">office cleaning in Hull</a> and nearby towns including <a href="{{area:hessle}}">Hessle</a> and <a href="{{area:cottingham}}">Cottingham</a>, with vetted, DBS-checked cleaners, eco-friendly products on request and fixed quotes agreed before we start. If you're reviewing your current arrangements, our guide to <a href="{{guide:choosing-a-commercial-cleaning-company}}">choosing a commercial cleaning contractor</a> may help.</p>
<p>To talk about your premises, see our <a href="{{service:office-cleaning}}">office cleaning service</a> or <a href="{{page:contact}}">get in touch with our team</a>.</p>
